RADIUS (Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service) is a networking protocol designed for centralized access management and accounting of users connecting to or using a network service. 
It provides Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) services, enabling network administrators to control user access to various network resources. 
This process is centrally managed, and a key feature of RADIUS is its ability to handle user access to diverse network environments, including VPNs, Wi-Fi, and cloud services, through a single, central server.
The benefits of employing a RADIUS server encompass robust security, scalability, and granular monitoring of user activities. 
RADIUS utilizes encryption to safeguard sensitive information such as usernames and passwords, establishing it as a foundational standard in security systems.

